.TH UPSPIN 1
.SH NAME
upspin \- content-addressed, signed name space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B upspin
.RB [ -b
.IR base ]
.B keygen
.I user
.I skfile
.br
.B upspin
.RB [ -b
.IR base ]
.B keyadd
.I user
.I pkfile
.br
.B upspin
.RB [ -b
.IR base ]
.B put
.I name
.I file
.I skfile
.br
.B upspin
.RB [ -b
.IR base ]
.B get
.I name
.RI [ outfile ]
.br
.B upspin
.RB [ -b
.IR base ]
.B ls
.I owner
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Upspin
stores content in a global, verifiable name space modelled on
.I upspin.io.
It is built from three parts:
.IP \(bu
a
.B Store
of content-addressed blocks \(em each block is named by the SHA-256 of
its bytes, and is verified against that name every time it is read;
.IP \(bu
a
.B Dir
mapping an owner-rooted global
.I name
of the form
.IB user @ domain / path
to a signed directory entry that lists the blocks making up the content;
.IP \(bu
a
.B KeyServer
mapping
.IB user @ domain
to a public key.
.PP
To write, content is split into fixed-size blocks stored in the Store,
and a directory entry naming those blocks is signed with the owner's
secret key and written to the Dir.
To read, the entry is looked up, its owner is taken from the
.IR name ,
that owner's key is fetched from the KeyServer, and the signature is
verified against it \(em so trust rests on the KeyServer binding of name
to key, never on a key carried inside the entry.
Each block is then fetched and checked against its content hash.
A tampered block, a tampered entry, or an entry signed by anyone other
than the name's owner is therefore rejected.
.PP
.I Base
holds the
.BR store/ ,
.B dir/
and
.B keys/
subtrees; it defaults to
.B $UPSPINROOT
or
.BR /mnt/upspin .
.SS Commands
.TP
.B keygen
Generate a key pair for
.IR user ,
writing the secret key to
.I skfile
and registering the public key in the KeyServer.
.TP
.B keyadd
Register the public key in
.I pkfile
for
.I user
in the KeyServer.
.TP
.B put
Store
.I file
under
.I name
(which must be
.IB user @ domain / path\fR),
signed with the secret key in
.IR skfile .
.TP
.B get
Fetch, verify and write the content of
.I name
to
.I outfile
or standard output.
.TP
.B ls
List the names owned by
.IR owner .
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX

	upspin keygen ann@example.com ann.key
	upspin put ann@example.com/notes/todo todo.txt ann.key
	upspin get ann@example.com/notes/todo
	upspin ls ann@example.com

.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/upspin.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/upspin.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR upspin (2),
.IR keyring (2),
.IR pkg (1)
.PP
.B /doc/pkg-roadmap.md
.SH BUGS
The Store, Dir and KeyServer are local directories; serving them over
.IR styx (2)
so names resolve across the network, and end-to-end encryption (per-reader
wrapped keys and Access files) are not yet done.
The signature digest is SHA-1 (the
.IR keyring (2)
certificate algorithm); block content addresses are SHA-256.
Names must not contain newlines.
